    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Cal Raleigh** (74.07) and **Geraldo Perdomo** (72.67) anchor the lineup, benefiting from highly favorable matchups against suspect pitching rotations. **Cal Raleigh** stands out as the primary offensive engine; despite a slight park penalty, his engagement against a high-ERA arm like Abbott provides a significant floor-ceiling combination. Similarly, **Geraldo Perdomo** benefits from being slotted in the #2 hole against Springs, maximizing his opportunities to contribute across the 5x5 categories. **Daulton Varsho** (71.62) also ranks among our elite starters today; his ability to exploit a platoon advantage against Martinez while maintaining high stolen-base potential makes him a critical asset for our scarcity-focused strategy.\n\nThe decision to keep **Masyn Winn** (50.84) on the bench was our most difficult call of the day. While **Masyn Winn** maintains a solid projection and high sprint speed, he ultimately fell short of the threshold required to displace starters like **Gavin Sheets** or **Yainer Diaz**, both of whom benefit from superior daily efficiency scores derived from their respective matchup dynamics. Elsewhere, we are leaning heavily into **Taylor Ward** (64.85) and **Luis Arraez** (64.37), both of whom benefit from optimal batting order placement\u2014#1 in their respective lineups\u2014and favorable platoon splits. \n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the risk profile of **Jonathan Aranda** (52.24). While the projection rewards his platoon edge against a righty, he is facing Cease, an elite arm with a low 2.96 xERA, which substantially suppresses his ceiling today. Furthermore, while **Jeremiah Jackson** (45.24) earns a spot due to roster constraints, his score is depressed by a bottom-tier batting order position and a modest holding risk regarding stolen bases against the opposing battery. Ensure all lineups are confirmed before first pitch, as any mid-day shuffling could impact these aggressive order-dependent projections.",
